  "In the usual polynomial-time notion of efficiency, the reduction itself must take polynomial time in the original input length. Otherwise, the combined method may remain expensive even with a fast solver for the target problem. Stronger goals need tighter accounting: a quadratic reduction would not support a claimed linear-time total algorithm merely because it is polynomial.",
  "A recursion tree displays the number, sizes, and local costs of calls at each depth. Sum the work across each level to see whether costs grow, shrink, or remain comparable. This can reveal whether the root, leaves, or all levels determine the total. Tree depth alone is insufficient because a shallow level may contain many expensive calls.",
