  "The original request might ask whether a whole list contains a subset summing to a target. Recursive calls need the more general question for an arbitrary remaining suffix and residual target. Defining that broader contract makes every call meaningful. The original problem then becomes one initial call, while the generalized state supports the smaller instances used throughout the search.",
  "Keep all information that can affect future legality or outcomes, while avoiding irrelevant history when possible. For subset sum, an item index and residual target suffice for feasibility with a fixed list. For chess-like rules, a board alone may not suffice. State design is a correctness decision first; reducing the state for efficiency is safe only when the omitted information cannot change the continuation answer.",
  "To ensure completeness, enumerate every next choice consistent with the current state. For a subset decision, those choices are including or excluding the next item. Each possible solution follows one of those branches. You may stop after finding a witness for a yes-or-no problem, but you cannot discard an untried branch before failure unless a valid pruning argument rules it out.",
