  "For an existence question, one successful continuation is enough, so alternative choices combine with logical OR. If either including or excluding the next item can reach the target, the current state is feasible. For counting or optimization, the combination changes to a sum, minimum, or maximum as appropriate. Choose it from the question being asked, not from the fact that the algorithm branches.",
  "Pruning is safe only when the partial assignment cannot lead to a needed solution. In a positive-number subset-sum instance, a negative remaining target rules out success because later additions cannot undo the excess. If negative input values are allowed, that same rule becomes invalid. Every pruning condition depends on assumptions that should be explicit in the problem specification.",
  "A recursive solver often handles more states than the user's initial question mentions. Text segmentation starts with the whole string but recursively asks about arbitrary suffixes. Define the solver for those suffixes so its contract matches all calls. Generalizing the problem in this controlled way is what makes recursion reusable, rather than a collection of special cases for the original input.",
  "If two paths reach the same complete future-relevant state, the remaining feasibility or optimal continuation is identical. This justifies reusing a cached answer. The key word is complete: available items, remaining budget, and turn information may all matter. Caching by only one visible parameter can return an answer computed under different constraints and silently make the search incorrect.",
