  "In the basic problem, future word choices depend only on the unprocessed suffix and the fixed dictionary. Earlier words do not restrict later ones, so their exact sequence can be forgotten for feasibility. If the problem added grammatical constraints or a limit on word count, more state might be necessary. The sufficiency of the suffix follows from this particular contract.",
  "The empty string needs no words to cover it, so it has a valid segmentation consisting of zero words. This makes it the successful endpoint of recursion after the last chosen word consumes all remaining characters. Returning false there would reject every complete segmentation. The base case expresses completion, not the claim that an empty string is itself a dictionary word.",
  "When recursion reaches the empty suffix, every character has already been covered by valid earlier choices. Returning true tells the caller that its chosen final word completed the task. Candidate words should be nonempty so each recursive step advances. Otherwise, selecting an empty word could repeatedly call the solver on the same suffix without reaching a new state.",
  "A segmentation must cover the string from its first character through its last using adjacent valid words. It cannot skip an inconvenient letter or choose overlapping pieces. For 'sunflower', 'sun' followed by 'flower' is a candidate if both belong to the dictionary. The recursive formulation enforces full coverage by continuing on exactly the suffix after each chosen prefix.",
  "Choosing the first split position determines the first word and the remaining suffix. For a suffix of length m, consider endpoints one through m, subject to dictionary validity. Every complete segmentation appears under one of those choices. This turns an open-ended question about all possible word sequences into a precise recursive decision at each call.",
  "A prefix is usable only if the dictionary predicate accepts it. The search may examine many character prefixes, but only accepted ones justify recursive continuation. Keep that membership test distinct from suffix feasibility: a real word can still lead to a dead end. Together, the two checks ensure every selected segment is allowed and the entire string is eventually covered.",
  "Suppose 'an' is a dictionary word. That alone does not prove 'android' is segmentable; the remaining 'droid' must also have a valid decomposition. The recurrence therefore uses an AND within a candidate branch: accepted prefix and successful suffix. It then uses OR across different prefix choices, since any complete decomposition is enough.",
  "Each possible first word offers an alternative way to begin. If even one choice leaves a segmentable suffix, the whole string is segmentable, so the alternatives combine with OR. A solver may return immediately after finding such a choice. If the task instead counted all decompositions, early return and OR would lose information and would need to be replaced by counting logic.",
