  "A root at position r splits interval i through j into i through r minus one and r plus one through j. These are smaller ordered intervals, so the same optimization problem applies recursively. Empty intervals are allowed at the ends. This state structure explains why two endpoints are enough to identify a subproblem when the key order and frequencies remain fixed.",
  "Every search in the interval pays one comparison at the current root before it either stops or continues into a subtree. A nonroot key's remaining comparisons are counted in its subtree cost, while the root key has none left. Thus adding the interval-frequency sum accounts for exactly one comparison per search; it does not mean a root search descends below the root.",
  "With no keys in an interval, there are no successful key searches to charge, and no root to choose. Returning zero lets an endpoint root combine with an empty side naturally. Returning an impossible-state marker instead would wrongly reject valid trees with missing children. The zero is a legitimate empty contribution, unlike an infeasible optimization branch.",
  "The recurrence must consider every legal root position because choosing one determines both depth costs and the remaining intervals. A root that looks attractive by frequency alone can force expensive placements elsewhere. For each candidate, solve both interval subproblems and compare the complete totals. This exhaustive choice is what supports the optimum guarantee before any proven optimization is introduced.",
  "After fixing the root, left-side choices do not change which keys belong on the right or their frequencies. The total cost separates into left cost, right cost, and a fixed root-comparison term. Therefore each subtree can be optimized independently. Independence applies after the root is fixed; selecting the root itself still requires comparing the combined consequences on both sides.",
  "Suppose an allegedly optimal tree contains a subtree that is not optimal for its interval. Replace that subtree with a cheaper valid one on the same keys. Its attachment depth stays fixed, so all unchanged contributions remain unchanged while the subtree's internal cost falls. The whole tree becomes cheaper, contradicting the original claim and establishing optimal substructure.",
