  "CircuitSat asks whether there exists any assignment of true and false values to the input wires that makes the output true. Evaluating one assignment answers a different question: what happens for those particular bits? For a circuit computing x AND y, both inputs true provide a satisfying assignment. A circuit computing x AND NOT x has none.",
  "Each of n binary inputs has two possible values, so independent choices multiply to 2 to the n complete assignments. A brute-force search can test them one by one. It may stop early after finding a satisfying assignment, but an unsatisfiable circuit requires exhausting the list under this strategy. The count concerns assignments, not the cost of evaluating each one.",
  "Once input bits are fixed, process the acyclic circuit in dependency order, computing each gate after its inputs are known. With ordinary bounded-size gates, this takes time proportional to the circuit representation, including gates and wires. Shared subcircuits should be evaluated once rather than recursively recomputed for every use. Fast evaluation does not automatically give a fast method for finding satisfying inputs.",
  "Enumerating all assignments involves 2 to the n candidates for n input bits. Multiply that count by the cost of evaluating one circuit to bound the straightforward search. This is exponential dependence on the number of inputs. It is an upper bound for a particular method, not a proof that every algorithm must inspect every assignment in an explicit circuit.",
  "A satisfying assignment is a witness because anyone can plug in its bits and evaluate the circuit to confirm the yes answer. Its length is the number of input variables, so it is short relative to the explicit circuit description. Verification does not require discovering the assignment. This distinction between checking and finding is central to the definition of NP.",
  "General CircuitSat is NP-complete, so a polynomial-time solver would establish P equals NP. That question remains unresolved; the lack of a known polynomial solver is not a lower-bound proof. Some circuits and practical instances are easy, and specialized methods can outperform naive enumeration. The open claim concerns a polynomial-time guarantee for every explicit circuit, not whether useful solving techniques exist.",
  "A decision problem asks a yes-or-no question. CircuitSat returns yes if at least one input assignment makes the output true and no otherwise. Producing an actual satisfying assignment is a related search task, but the decision definition only asks whether one exists. Keeping those formulations distinct makes complexity-class and certificate claims precise.",
  "With three switches, the settings are 000 through 111, giving eight possibilities. Each additional independent binary switch doubles the number, so n switches give 2 to the n settings. In the opaque-box story, the only information comes from testing settings. That restricted access is crucial to the adversary's argument about how many tests may be necessary.",
  "An explicit circuit reveals its gates and connections, allowing an evaluator to propagate chosen input values through it. Each gate's output follows from already computed inputs in an acyclic dependency order. This can be done in linear time in the circuit size. The diagram may also reveal structural information unavailable in an opaque box, which is why black-box lower bounds do not automatically transfer.",
  "To certify a yes instance, provide one value for each input wire. The verifier checks that the assignment is well formed and evaluates the circuit. A true output confirms satisfiability; a false output rejects that proposed witness but does not establish unsatisfiability. Another assignment might work, so the verifier's rejection and the decision problem's no answer are different statements.",
  "One working assignment proves satisfiability immediately after evaluation. A failed assignment proves only that this particular choice does not work. Establishing unsatisfiability must rule out every assignment, perhaps through structural reasoning or a proof system. Some unsatisfiable circuits have simple explanations, but no general polynomial-size, polynomially checkable certificate scheme is known for all of them.",
  "An opaque box lets you observe outputs for selected inputs but hides how those outputs are computed. An explicit circuit supplies the full gate structure, which an algorithm may inspect and exploit. A circuit containing an obvious contradiction can be rejected without enumerating every assignment. Therefore a query argument for the box models less access than the ordinary CircuitSat input provides.",
  "In the black-box game, untested settings can remain consistent with a hidden satisfying input, forcing many queries in the worst case. With an explicit circuit, the algorithm can analyze its structure instead of only querying outputs. The black-box argument does not rule out such methods. A lower bound must match the information and operations allowed by the actual computational model.",
  "AND produces true when both inputs are true, OR when at least one is true, and NOT reverses a truth value. Connecting these gates in an acyclic network defines a Boolean function of the input bits. CircuitSat asks whether that function ever returns true. The circuit structure can share intermediate results, so it need not be a tree-shaped formula.",
  "The circuit's representation includes its operations and their connections. Counting only input variables misses the work needed to evaluate a circuit with many gates. Use separate parameters when helpful: n input bits give 2 to the n assignments, while circuit size determines the cost of evaluating each assignment. Complexity should ultimately be measured against the encoded input length.",
  "The brute-force solver enumerates complete input assignments and evaluates the circuit on each. It returns yes as soon as one produces true; if none does, it returns no after enumeration finishes. This is correct because every possible witness is considered. Its weakness is the number of assignments, which doubles with each additional input bit.",
  "Testing all assignments gives an upper bound of O(2 to the n times s), where n is the number of input bits and s is the circuit size under linear evaluation. An upper bound describes an available algorithm, not a requirement imposed on all algorithms. Faster behavior on special structures or many practical instances is compatible with the absence of a general polynomial guarantee.",
