  "Accessing a file requires passing every earlier file and reading the requested one. A long file near the beginning therefore adds its length to many requests, not just its own. Moving it later can spare shorter files that delay. Under equal request frequencies, this repeated contribution explains why sorting from shortest to longest reduces average access cost.",
  "Suppose equally likely files have lengths two and nine. Short-first completion times are two and eleven, averaging 6.5; long-first times are nine and eleven, averaging ten. An adjacent-swap argument extends this reasoning to any list: every longer-before-shorter inversion can be improved. Therefore a nondecreasing length order minimizes the average under the tape model.",
  "For adjacent files of lengths a greater than b, swapping the short file ahead saves a units on its request and adds b units on the long file's request. Later files still have the same total length before them. With equal request probability, the net change is proportional to b minus a, which is negative. Thus the swap improves the average.",
  "Take any arrangement with a longer file immediately before a shorter one. Swapping that pair reduces cost while leaving all other requests unchanged. Repeatedly remove such inversions until the files are sorted by length. Since no swap increases cost, the sorted order is at least as good as every starting arrangement, turning a local comparison into an optimality proof.",
  "The shortest-first rule feels plausible because early files delay later ones, but the exchange calculation makes that intuition checkable. It shows exactly how the two affected request costs change and why other files are unaffected. Not every greedy rule has this property. Here the swap proof establishes the rule's correctness under the model's equal-frequency and restart assumptions.",
  "A file requested a thousand times may deserve an early position even if it is long. Compare both length and frequency rather than applying shortest-first blindly. For positive frequencies, the optimal ordering uses increasing length divided by frequency. The pairwise swap calculation weighs each delay by how often the affected file is accessed, which explains the ratio.",
  "In this model, a request starts at the tape's beginning and finishes after scanning through the requested file. Its cost is therefore the sum of lengths up to and including that file. A file's own length is unavoidable for its request, but its position determines how much preceding data must also be traversed. Reordering changes those completion positions.",
  "With equal frequencies, putting a shorter file first delays the longer file by a smaller amount than the reverse order delays the shorter one. The combined completion cost decreases by the length difference. This pairwise comparison remains valid with any unchanged prefix before the pair, so it can be applied throughout the list to justify the global shortest-first order.",
  "An adjacent exchange is easy to analyze because only the two files' individual completion times change. Every later file still follows the same combined length, and every earlier file is untouched. This isolates the cost difference and avoids comparing whole permutations at once. Repeated safe exchanges can then transform an arbitrary arrangement into the proposed greedy order.",
  "Multiply each file's completion time by its request count and add the results to obtain total access cost. If the weights are probabilities instead, the same expression gives expected cost. Normalizing counts by their total changes only a common factor, so it does not change the best ordering. The frequencies make delays to popular files more expensive.",
  "For adjacent files a and b, placing a first is no worse precisely when length(a) times frequency(b) is at most length(b) times frequency(a). With positive frequencies, this is the increasing length/frequency rule. Cross-multiplication avoids floating-point ratio comparisons. Positive-length files with zero access frequency can be placed last, since they otherwise delay requested files without benefiting their own accesses.",
  "For this sorting-based proof, identify an inversion of the proposed order and show swapping it never increases cost. Repeating the swap removes all inversions and reaches a sorted arrangement. This establishes global optimality because every initial order can be transformed without harm. A pairwise rule without the cost-preservation argument would only specify a sorting preference, not prove it solves the objective.",
  "A request for the third file pays for scanning the first two as well as reading the third. Its cost is not just the third file's length. This cumulative structure is why order affects the objective. A long early file appears in many later prefix sums, so moving it can change many requests even though the total tape length remains unchanged.",
  "If adjacent files have the same length and equal request frequency, exchanging them gives the same two completion positions and the same total cost. Their names do not affect the objective. Ties can therefore be ordered arbitrarily in this model. Unequal frequencies change the comparison: among equal-length files, the more frequently requested file should come first.",
  "Consider a length-ten file requested one hundred times and a length-one file requested once. Their length/frequency ratios are 0.1 and 1, so the long popular file belongs first. The frequent request savings outweigh the rare file's added delay. This shows why shortest-first is specific to equal frequencies rather than a universal tape-ordering rule.",
  "A local swap proves something global when every unsorted arrangement contains an improvable inversion and swaps eventually reach the proposed order. For tape files, swapping an inverted adjacent pair changes only their weighted completion costs. Once all inversions are removed, the ratio-sorted order cannot be worse than the original arrangement. The proof compares against every possible starting order, not just nearby candidates.",
  "After deriving the correct ordering key, a comparison sort finds the order in O(n log n) comparisons. For equal frequencies the key is length; for positive unequal frequencies it is length/frequency. This analyzes arranging the file references. Actually writing the files to tape also costs time proportional to the amount of data written and must be included if that operation is part of the task.",
  "For an order with lengths two, five, and nine, the prefix sums are two, seven, and sixteen. Those are the modeled completion times for requests to the first, second, and third files. Summing or frequency-weighting these values gives the objective. Prefix sums make explicit how an early file contributes to the access cost of every file after it.",
