  "Each edge on a root-to-leaf path contributes one bit, so a symbol at depth d has a d-bit codeword. A frequent symbol benefits from a shallow leaf because that shorter code is used repeatedly. The code tree need not preserve alphabetical order among symbols, unlike a binary search tree, which changes the optimization problem substantially.",
  "If a symbol occurs f times and its codeword has length d, it contributes f times d bits to the encoded message. Add that contribution over all symbols to get total payload length. For example, shortening a symbol used one hundred times by one bit saves one hundred bits. Huffman coding minimizes this weighted length among binary prefix-free codes for the given frequencies.",
  "Huffman's algorithm selects the two smallest active frequencies and makes their trees siblings under a new parent. The merged tree returns to the active set with their combined frequency. Repeating this eventually forms one code tree. The greedy choice is justified by showing that some optimal tree can place those two least frequent symbols as deepest siblings.",
  "When two symbols or subtrees merge, every occurrence of either follows the new parent's path before taking its final branch. The parent's effective frequency is therefore the sum of their frequencies. If weights are three and five, the merged weight is eight. This combined weight correctly represents how often later increases in the subtree's depth will add a bit.",
  "A shorter codeword saves one bit on every occurrence of its symbol. Assigning that saving to a frequent symbol usually reduces total length more than assigning it to a rare one. The exact code lengths must still form a valid prefix-free tree. Huffman's algorithm balances those structural constraints while minimizing the frequency-weighted length.",
  "A decoder follows bits from the tree's root until reaching a leaf, emits that symbol, and starts again at the root. Because no leaf path is a prefix of another codeword, it need not wait for a separator to distinguish completion. The decoder still needs the code definition and appropriate message framing; prefix freedom removes between-symbol delimiter ambiguity.",
  "Label left edges 0 and right edges 1. Reading those labels from the root to a symbol's leaf produces its codeword. Swapping left and right children changes bit patterns but not code lengths or weighted cost. This explains why different Huffman trees or branch conventions can encode the same frequency distribution equally efficiently.",
  "The objective counts actual bits contributed by each symbol: frequency multiplied by codeword length. A code with one very long rare symbol may beat a balanced code if it shortens several common symbols. Compare weighted totals rather than average leaf depth without frequencies. The standard objective excludes separate overhead for storing the codebook, which may matter for short practical messages.",
  "In a full optimal code tree, choose a deepest sibling pair of leaves. The two least frequent symbols can be exchanged into those positions without increasing weighted length, because deeper positions are cheapest for rarer symbols. Thus at least one optimal tree has the required sibling pair. The statement is existential; ties can produce other equally optimal arrangements.",
  "Replace the two chosen symbols by a single combined symbol whose weight is their sum. Solve the smaller coding problem, then expand that combined symbol into the original two children. Expansion adds one bit to each occurrence of either child, a fixed cost equal to the combined weight. This relation connects the smaller optimum to the original problem.",
  "Each merge removes two active trees and inserts one, reducing the active count by one. Starting with n symbols, n minus one merges leave a single tree when n is at least one. This supplies a clear termination measure. The one-symbol case may require a chosen encoding convention in a practical format, since its abstract tree has a zero-depth leaf.",
  "A merged item represents a whole subtree, not a new original message symbol. Its node becomes an internal parent with the two merged trees as children. Later merges can attach that parent under another node. Keeping these child links records the history needed to recover each original symbol's root-to-leaf codeword once construction finishes.",
  "A min-priority queue repeatedly removes the two smallest active weights and inserts their sum. With a binary heap, each merge uses logarithmic-time queue operations, giving O(n log n) construction time after initialization. Store child references alongside weights so the final code tree can be reconstructed. Ties need a consistent comparable representation but do not change optimal weighted length.",
