  "Moving a symbol one level closer to the root saves its frequency in total bits. That makes shallow placement especially valuable for common symbols. However, codewords cannot be shortened independently: the leaves must still form a prefix-free tree. The weighted objective and tree constraint together explain the shape Huffman's merges produce.",
  "If an internal node represented a symbol, its path would already be a complete codeword while also beginning every descendant codeword. The decoder could not determine whether to stop there using prefix freedom alone. Putting symbols at leaves avoids that conflict. Internal nodes instead represent partial prefixes shared by several possible symbols.",
