  "A blocking pair consists of two participants who are not matched together but each strictly prefers the other to their assigned partner. They would both rather leave the current matching for one another, making it unstable. One person's dissatisfaction is insufficient: the preference must be mutual. This definition assumes the standard two-sided setting and must be adjusted carefully when ties or unacceptable partners are allowed.",
  "Stability means there is no unmatched cross-side pair that would both prefer to be together. It does not promise everyone their first choice or maximize a sum of happiness scores. A matching can contain disappointed participants and still be stable if no mutually preferred alternative pair exists. Check every potential pair against both participants' current partners to test the definition.",
  "A free proposer approaches the highest-ranked receiver not yet tried. If rejected, the proposer moves down its list rather than repeating the same request. Receivers keep their best proposal so far, possibly releasing a former tentative partner. This process preserves progress through finite preference lists while allowing matches to improve on the receiving side.",
  "A receiver compares a new proposal with the current tentative partner and keeps whichever is preferred. If the newcomer wins, the old partner becomes free and continues proposing. Tentative acceptance is essential: permanently accepting the first offer can prevent stability. The receiver's sequence of partners only improves, a property used in the final correctness proof.",
  "Fixing one blocking pair by swapping partners may create a different blocking pair involving the displaced participants. Nothing in that naive rule guarantees steady progress, so it can revisit earlier matchings. Deferred acceptance uses a stronger structure: proposers never repeat a proposal and receivers only improve their held offers. Those properties support termination and stability proofs.",
  "Maximum bipartite matching asks for as many allowed pairs as possible, regardless of ranked preferences. Stable matching asks for a matching without mutually preferred deviations. In the standard complete equal-size setting, stable matchings are perfect, but stability is still a different requirement. A maximum-cardinality matching can be unstable if two participants prefer each other over their assigned partners.",
  "To identify a blocking pair, take two participants from opposite sides who are not currently paired. Compare each one's preference for the other against the assigned partner. Both must prefer the alternative. A participant who likes someone better does not alone create instability if that person prefers their existing match, which is why stability is a mutual condition.",
  "Tentative matches remain open to improvement on the receiving side. If receiver R holds proposer A but prefers new proposer B, R keeps B and rejects A. A then resumes at the next untried choice. This does not reverse R's earlier progress: R's held partner becomes strictly better under strict preferences, which prevents previously rejected proposers from becoming attractive later.",
  "A receiver rejects a proposer only while holding someone preferred, and later held partners are at least as good. The rejected proposer therefore cannot become preferable in a later round under fixed preferences. There is no benefit to proposing there again. Recording the rejection also bounds total proposals because each proposer-receiver pair is considered at most once.",
  "Each proposer maintains a position in its preference list. A proposal consumes the next untried receiver, whether it is accepted tentatively or rejected. If the proposer becomes free later, it continues from that position. This one-way movement through the list is a concrete progress measure, unlike arbitrary partner swaps that can return to earlier configurations.",
  "With n participants on each side, there are n squared possible proposer-receiver pairs. No pair receives the same proposal twice, so the algorithm makes at most n squared proposals. Under complete strict lists, the process ends in a perfect matching. The finite-proposal argument proves termination; a separate argument is needed to show the final matching has no blocking pair.",
  "A receiver keeps the preferred choice between the current partner and each new offer. Consequently, its tentative partner never becomes worse in its own ranking. This monotonic improvement explains why a past rejection remains decisive: the receiver will not later prefer that rejected proposer over its final partner. The property depends on preferences remaining fixed during the algorithm.",
  "Suppose proposer P prefers receiver R to its final partner. Because P proposes in preference order, P must have tried R earlier and been rejected, immediately or later. R then held someone preferred to P and could only improve afterward. Therefore R prefers its final partner to P, contradicting the claim that P and R form a blocking pair.",
  "Stability is the absence of blocking pairs, not an objective that simply counts matches. In the standard complete equal-size model, the proposal algorithm matches everyone, but many perfect matchings may still be unstable. A separate maximum-matching algorithm ignores ranking conflicts. Choose the formulation according to whether the problem cares about capacity alone or about participants' preferences as well.",
  "Under strict complete preferences in the standard two-sided model, deferred acceptance gives each proposer its most preferred partner among all stable matchings. This does not mean every proposer gets its overall first choice; some preferred partners cannot appear with that proposer in any stable matching. Reversing which side proposes can therefore change the outcome and favor the other side.",
  "Before receiving any proposal, a receiver can remain unmatched. Once a proposal is accepted, the receiver always holds some tentative partner, replacing it only with a preferred one. In the complete equal-size model, an unmatched receiver means some proposer has not yet exhausted the relevant possibilities. This observation helps prove that termination leaves every participant matched.",
  "An arbitrary blocking-pair swap can improve the new pair's situation while worsening the displaced partners', potentially recreating earlier conflicts. There is no built-in measure that must move in one direction. Deferred acceptance instead bounds proposals and preserves receiver improvement. The contrast explains why local repairs require a termination argument rather than an assumption that repeated improvement must eventually settle.",
  "The standard algorithm uses a fixed strict ranking of the opposite side for each participant. Proposers move down their lists, and receivers compare offers using their own rankings. Complete equal-size lists support the usual perfect-matching theorem. Variants with ties, incomplete lists, or capacities require explicit definitions and corresponding guarantees rather than silently inheriting every conclusion of the basic model.",
  "After rejection, a proposer tries the next receiver not previously approached. The rejected receiver need not be retried because it already holds a preferred offer and can only improve. Maintaining the next-choice index makes this behavior efficient and ensures progress. If all acceptable options are exhausted in an incomplete-list variant, the proposer may remain unmatched instead of continuing indefinitely.",
