  "Let the current forest be extendable to an MST, and take a component's vertex set as a cut. A minimum-weight edge leaving it is safe to add by the cut exchange argument. With tied weights, it belongs to some compatible MST, not necessarily every MST. Distinct weights remove that ambiguity, but the general extendability invariant works without requiring uniqueness.",
  "A safe edge is one whose addition leaves the selected set contained in at least one MST. This is stronger than simply avoiding a cycle. By maintaining that invariant until the selected edges form a spanning tree, the algorithm can conclude the final tree is minimum. The proof must justify safety for the particular cut and selected set at each step.",
  "Add a candidate light cut edge to an MST that does not contain it. The added edge creates one cycle, and that cycle contains another edge crossing the same cut. Removing the other crossing edge restores a spanning tree. Since the candidate is no heavier, cost does not increase; with strict inequality, it would contradict the original tree's claimed optimality.",
  "Distinct edge weights ensure that each relevant minimum crossing edge is unique and imply a unique MST in a connected graph. If weights tie, multiple MSTs may exist, though ties do not force nonuniqueness. Use the weaker statement that a light cut edge can belong to an MST when uniqueness is unavailable. The algorithm still works with suitable tie handling.",
  "A spanning tree includes every vertex, connects them, and contains no cycle. Connectivity provides a path between any two vertices; acyclicity makes that path unique. For a graph with V vertices, a spanning tree has V minus one edges. A minimum spanning tree additionally minimizes the sum of edge weights among all such spanning trees.",
  "Initially the empty edge set is contained in every spanning tree. Each safe addition preserves containment in some MST, so the forest never commits to an edge set that rules out optimality. Once it becomes connected, it is itself a spanning tree and must be minimum. This invariant is the reason greedy choices can accumulate without backtracking.",
  "Within a forest component, the existing selected edges already provide a unique path between any two vertices. Adding an edge between them creates a cycle consisting of that path and the new edge. Rejecting such edges preserves acyclicity. It does not alone ensure optimality, because an expensive edge between different components can still be a poor choice.",
  "A cut partitions the vertices into a set S and its complement. An edge crosses the cut when exactly one endpoint belongs to S. Every spanning tree of a connected graph must cross each nontrivial cut, or it would fail to connect the two sides. This necessity is what makes comparing crossing-edge weights useful in MST proofs.",
  "For an edge to be safe relative to selected forest F, choose a cut that does not split any selected edge. A minimum-weight edge crossing that cut can be added while preserving extension to some MST. The 'appropriate' condition matters: a cut that crosses already selected edges does not directly support the same invariant-preserving argument.",
  "A tree already has one path between the endpoints of an added edge. Including that edge creates exactly one cycle: the old path plus the new edge. The exchange proof removes a suitable edge from this cycle, restoring a tree. When the new edge crosses a cut, the old path must cross it too, supplying an exchange candidate.",
  "A safe edge minimizes weight among edges crossing a particular cut, not necessarily among every edge in the graph. A globally lighter edge may lie entirely within one side and do nothing to connect that cut. Compare the correct candidate set. Jarnik's algorithm uses the cut around its growing tree, while other MST algorithms choose cuts differently.",
  "If two different MSTs existed with all edge weights distinct, a lightest differing-edge exchange would improve one of them, contradicting minimality. Thus distinctness is sufficient for uniqueness. It is not necessary: a graph that is already a tree has only one spanning tree even if several weights tie. Keep the direction of the guarantee clear.",
  "Take all vertices in one current forest component as S and all remaining vertices as the other side. No selected edge crosses this cut, because selected edges stay within components. Therefore a minimum outgoing edge is eligible for the safe-edge argument. If the original graph is connected and more than one component remains, at least one outgoing edge exists.",
  "A disconnected graph has no spanning tree covering all vertices because no edge can join its separate components. Instead, compute an MST within each connected component; their union is a minimum spanning forest. An algorithm that stops after exploring one start component must restart elsewhere to produce that full forest, rather than claiming it has spanned the entire disconnected graph.",
  "MST algorithms do not rely on the vague idea that small edges are always good. Their selected set must remain extendable to an optimum, typically justified through the cut property. A cheap edge forming a cycle is still rejected, and a merely cycle-free edge may be unsafe. The invariant explains exactly which greedy commitments preserve the possibility of a minimum final tree.",
  "If replacing one tree edge with a lighter candidate preserves connectivity and acyclicity, the resulting spanning tree has smaller total weight. That contradicts the claim that the original was minimum. The structural preservation is essential: replacing an arbitrary edge could disconnect the graph. The cycle-and-cut argument identifies a replacement edge that makes the contradiction valid.",
  "A tree on V vertices has V minus one edges. Starting with V isolated components, every edge joining two different components reduces the component count by one. Reaching one component therefore requires exactly V minus one such additions. If a connected selected subgraph has more edges, it contains a cycle and is not a tree.",
  "The disjoint-set find operation identifies each endpoint's current forest component. Different representatives mean the edge joins two components, so adding it and calling union preserves acyclicity. Equal representatives mean the edge would create a cycle. This check alone does not prove minimum weight; an algorithm such as Kruskal also needs an appropriate weight-based selection order."
