  "Whatever-first search starts at a vertex and repeatedly explores neighbors of reached vertices. The bag's policy changes the order, but the basic result is the same: exactly the reachable vertices are visited. For an undirected graph, these form the start vertex's connected component. The generic guarantee is reachability; shortest paths require a more specific removal policy and proof.",
  "The bag holds pending exploration candidates. Processing one unvisited vertex adds its neighbors, extending the search frontier. A stack, queue, or priority queue can provide different removal orders. In the textbook's mark-on-removal version, the bag may contain duplicates or candidates already visited through another entry, so the visited check must occur before expanding them.",
  "In this version, a vertex is marked when first removed from the bag, immediately before its neighbors are added. Later copies are skipped. Other correct traversal implementations mark on insertion instead, so this timing is specific to the displayed framework. The important effect is that a vertex's adjacency list is expanded only once despite multiple possible routes to it.",
  "Store a candidate as a pair containing the visited predecessor and the neighbor it offers. When that neighbor is first accepted, record the predecessor as its parent. Following parent pointers then reconstructs a route to the start. With duplicate candidates, the accepted entry determines the parent; it need not be the first entry ever inserted for that vertex under every bag policy.",
  "Every visited vertex except the start receives one parent that was visited earlier. Following parents must therefore eventually reach the start without cycling. These edges connect all reached vertices and give exactly one incoming parent edge per nonroot vertex, forming a tree. It spans the reachable component, though its paths are not necessarily shortest under an arbitrary bag policy.",
  "The bag decides which pending candidate is explored next. A last-in-first-out stack encourages deep exploration, while a first-in-first-out queue preserves breadth layers. Both can reach the same component while producing different visitation orders and parent trees. Additional guarantees depend on the policy; the generic search proof alone establishes only that reachable vertices are found.",
  "Put the start vertex in the bag, repeatedly remove a candidate, and expand it if it has not been visited. Each expansion adds adjacent vertices as further candidates. This process follows actual graph edges, so it cannot invent a route to another disconnected component. Once the finite bag is exhausted, all reachable candidates have been accounted for.",
  "Conceptually the bag is the search's unfinished work. A neighbor becomes a candidate when reached from an expanded vertex, but it may wait while other candidates are processed. In the textbook's implementation, duplicate entries can remain even after that vertex is visited through another route. The visited guard distinguishes useful pending work from these stale candidates.",
  "A cycle can cause the same vertex to be offered repeatedly from different neighbors. Marking it and checking the mark before expansion ensures its neighbors are processed only once. The bag may still contain duplicates, but they are skipped when removed. This prevents repeated exploration from turning a finite graph traversal into an endless walk around a cycle.",
  "The parent pointer records the edge through which a vertex is accepted into the traversal tree. It connects that vertex to an already visited predecessor, providing a concrete explanation of reachability. The start has no parent. In a bag implementation that allows duplicate offers, assign the pointer when the winning candidate is processed and do not overwrite it on later stale entries.",
  "Each nonstart visited vertex attaches to one earlier visited vertex, so parent links cannot form a cycle. Repeatedly following them reaches the start, establishing connectivity. The resulting edges therefore form a tree spanning all reached vertices. Changing the exploration policy may change this tree while leaving the reached vertex set unchanged.",
  "In an undirected graph, vertices outside the start component have no path from the start. A traversal that only follows graph edges cannot reach them, regardless of how its bag is ordered. To explore the whole graph, start additional searches from unvisited vertices. The limitation is structural, not a failure of depth-first or breadth-first ordering.",
  "Initially the start is reachable by a path of zero edges. Whenever an expanded reachable vertex offers a neighbor, appending that edge gives a path to the neighbor. Thus every accepted vertex is reachable. This induction proves the search never visits an unrelated vertex, while a separate completeness argument shows it eventually visits every vertex that is reachable.",
  "Suppose a reachable vertex remained unvisited after the bag emptied. Along a path from the start, take the first unvisited vertex; its predecessor was visited and therefore offered it as a candidate. That candidate would eventually be removed and visited unless already visited, a contradiction. This proof depends on considering every relevant outgoing edge when expanding a vertex.",
  "A vertex reachable through several neighbors may acquire different parents depending on which candidate is processed first. The reached set remains the same, but the traversal tree can change. A queue imposes enough order to guarantee minimum-edge parent paths; a generic bag does not. Distinguish the invariant result of reachability from policy-dependent properties of the recorded tree.",
  "The generic bag needs insertion and removal, but its choice of removed item defines a search strategy. A stack produces depth-first behavior, a queue breadth-first behavior, and a priority queue a family of best-first methods. Operation costs also differ. The framework shares a reachability argument, while timing and stronger path guarantees depend on the chosen implementation.",
