  "A stack removes the newest pending candidate first. After expanding a vertex, newly added neighbors therefore take precedence over older unfinished branches, leading the search deeper before backtracking. Recursive DFS achieves the same effect through its call stack. Exact visitation order depends on neighbor ordering and implementation details, but the last-in-first-out discipline drives the depth-first structure.",
  "A queue removes candidates in the order they arrived. Neighbors discovered from the source are processed before neighbors discovered from those neighbors, creating layers by edge count. This is breadth-first search. The queue must support true first-in-first-out behavior; removing from the same end used for insertion would instead give a stack and lose the layer-order argument.",
  "BFS first reaches distance-one vertices, then distance-two vertices, and so on. A vertex's parent comes from the preceding layer, so following parent pointers gives a path with the fewest edges from the source. Neighbor order can select among equally short routes. The guarantee concerns edge count, not arbitrary edge-weight totals.",
  "A priority queue chooses the pending candidate with the best assigned priority, creating a best-first search. The priority formula determines the algorithm's meaning: edge weight supports an MST method, while cumulative distance supports a shortest-path method under suitable assumptions. The queue by itself does not identify which optimization problem is being solved or prove that the chosen priority is safe.",
  "For a candidate edge from u to v in a nonnegative-weight shortest-path search, use the known distance to u plus the edge weight. Prioritizing only the edge weight would ignore how expensive it was to reach u. This cumulative quantity measures the whole proposed route to v. The settled-distance guarantee requires the nonnegative-weight assumptions of the corresponding algorithm.",
  "A path's width is its smallest edge capacity: every unit traveling along the path must pass through that bottleneck. Extending a path of width eight with an edge of capacity three produces width three. Therefore the update uses a minimum, while the objective chooses the maximum width among alternative paths. This differs from adding weights for ordinary shortest-path distance.",
  "A deque with popleft removes the oldest queued candidate, giving first-in-first-out order. In the complete traversal, append newly discovered neighbors at the opposite end and maintain visited information. That order processes vertices by minimum edge distance. The shown setup identifies the queue policy, but neighbor expansion and duplicate handling are still required for a functioning BFS.",
  "Last-in-first-out removal favors the most recently generated branch. The search follows that branch until it has no unvisited continuation, then returns to older pending work. This is the reason a stack supports DFS. It does not imply the resulting route to every vertex is shortest; the search prioritizes depth, not minimum number of edges.",
  "When BFS expands a vertex at distance d, its newly discovered neighbors receive distance d plus one. The queue keeps them behind candidates already waiting at distance d, so the search completes each layer before moving beyond it. This ordering supports the shortest-edge-count proof and explains why changing the queue to a stack changes more than just cosmetic visitation order.",
  "BFS treats every edge as one step. A one-edge route of weight one hundred is therefore preferred in edge count to a two-edge route totaling two. That is correct for hop minimization but wrong for minimum weighted cost. Equal positive edge weights make the two objectives agree; otherwise use a shortest-path algorithm appropriate to the weights.",
  "The source is reachable from itself without traversing any edge, so its BFS distance is zero. Each newly discovered neighbor is assigned one more than its predecessor's distance. This initialization makes the layer numbers match path lengths and gives parent reconstruction a natural endpoint. Unreachable vertices need a separate marker rather than distance zero.",
  "BFS cannot first discover a vertex through a longer route while a shorter route remains unexplored, because all vertices on the shorter route's preceding layer are processed earlier. This layer argument establishes the first-discovery distance. Marking on discovery avoids duplicate queue entries in a common implementation; the textbook's guarded candidate version reaches the same minimum-edge result with appropriate parent handling.",
  "DFS follows one branch before checking all alternatives, so it may reach a vertex through a long path before noticing a direct edge from the source. Its parent tree certifies reachability, not minimum distance. BFS's queue discipline is what supplies the missing layer order. Choose DFS for its structural traversal properties rather than assuming every search tree is a shortest-path tree.",
  "If a vertex has several unvisited neighbors, their processing order determines which branch DFS follows first. That can change discovery times, parents, and the tree's shape while leaving reachability unchanged. Algorithms using DFS should rely on properties valid for the chosen traversal, not one example's exact order, unless a deterministic neighbor order is explicitly required.",
  "Both BFS and DFS need a way to avoid expanding vertices repeatedly through cycles. A visited mark ensures each vertex's adjacency list is handled once, even if several paths offer it. The stack or queue chooses order, while the mark controls repetition. Removing the mark can make either strategy loop indefinitely on a cyclic graph.",
  "In standard BFS, pending vertices belong to at most two consecutive distance layers: the current layer and neighbors being added to the next. New candidates cannot jump ahead of older ones because of first-in-first-out removal. This queue invariant explains the distance order. Implementations retaining duplicate candidates may carry stale entries, which must be skipped without disrupting that reasoning.",
  "Replacing the bag with a priority queue changes which candidate is selected next according to a defined key. This can support several algorithms, but each stronger guarantee requires its own key and proof. A minimum edge key is not interchangeable with a minimum path-distance key. The shared graph-search framework does not erase the distinction between their objectives.",
  "For a finite graph, processing every offered unvisited vertex and considering all its relevant neighbors eventually reaches exactly the start's reachable set, regardless of bag order. Stack, queue, and priority policies differ in order and cost, but not this basic result. Specialized claims such as shortest paths require additional arguments beyond the generic reachability proof.",
  "With adjacency lists, BFS expands each reachable vertex once and examines each relevant edge a constant number of times. Constant-time queue operations then give O(V plus E) for a full traversal, including initialization. An adjacency matrix can require scanning a whole row per vertex, changing the bound to O(V squared). Representation is therefore part of the runtime claim.",
