  "The preorder timestamp records when DFS first enters a vertex, before recursively exploring its unseen neighbors. It therefore orders discovery events. A global counter gives each entry a distinct time. Discovery order alone does not identify ancestry: a vertex in an earlier completed branch can have a smaller timestamp without being an ancestor of a later vertex.",
  "The postorder timestamp is assigned when DFS has finished considering every outgoing edge and all recursive descendants have returned. It records completion rather than discovery. A parent finishes after its descendants, which is why reversing finish order is useful for topological sorting in a DAG. Keep entry and exit times distinct when reasoning about traversal structure.",
  "Recursive calls behave like properly nested parentheses. A child call must finish before its parent returns, so its active interval lies inside the parent's. Calls in separate completed branches have disjoint intervals. Partial overlap would require one call to return while a descendant remained active, which ordinary recursive DFS cannot do.",
  "An active vertex has been entered but its DFS call has not returned. It remains on the recursion stack while descendants are explored. A visited vertex may instead be finished and no longer active, so a single visited flag cannot express this distinction. Directed cycle detection uses the active status to recognize an edge back into the current ancestor chain.",
  "An ancestor's call begins before the descendant is invoked and waits for that descendant to return before finishing. Thus its entry time is earlier and its exit time later. This nesting provides a constant-time ancestry test after timestamps are available. It describes ancestry in the DFS forest, which is more specific than general graph reachability.",
  "One DFS call may not reach every vertex, especially in a disconnected or directed graph. DFSAll scans the vertex set and starts another traversal whenever it encounters an unmarked vertex. Existing marks prevent revisiting completed regions. The resulting parent structure is a forest, and the shared clock can timestamp all its trees consistently.",
  "Preorder records a vertex at the moment it is first accepted into DFS, before processing descendants. If DFS enters A and then recursively enters B, A has the earlier preorder time. The values depend on start and neighbor order. They summarize one chosen traversal rather than an intrinsic numbering permanently determined by the graph.",
  "DFS assigns a vertex's postorder time after all its outgoing edges have been considered and recursive calls have completed. An edge to an already visited vertex does not require a new call, but it is still examined. This exit event closes the vertex's active interval and places descendants before ancestors in the finish sequence.",
  "The active vertices are exactly the unfinished recursive calls, forming the current DFS ancestor chain. Once a call returns, its vertex remains visited but becomes finished. This difference matters when inspecting an edge: reaching an active ancestor closes a directed cycle, while reaching an arbitrary finished vertex does not supply the same witness.",
  "A descendant is entered during an ancestor's call and finishes before that call can return. Therefore its whole active interval is nested within the ancestor's interval. For proper ancestry, use strict entry and exit inequalities. Equal vertices have the same interval, so whether equality counts as ancestry depends on the convention being used.",
  "DFS completes one branch before continuing a separate branch, so calls that are not ancestor-related have disjoint active intervals. Combined with nesting for ancestors, this rules out partial overlap. The property follows from recursive control flow, not from the graph being acyclic: DFS still has nested call intervals even when the input contains cycles.",
  "A smaller entry time means only that the vertex was discovered earlier. It may already have finished before another branch begins. To establish ancestry, also check that its exit time is later than the proposed descendant's exit. Using both timestamps distinguishes a containing active interval from an earlier disjoint interval.",
  "Every time DFSAll starts at an unmarked vertex, that vertex becomes a new forest root. Recursive discoveries beneath it receive parent pointers and form one tree. In directed graphs, the number of these trees depends on traversal order and reachability; it is not generally the number of strongly connected components. The forest records how this particular search discovered vertices.",
  "When DFS follows an edge from v to an unseen w, it sets w's parent to v before recursing. That edge becomes a tree edge. Edges to already seen vertices are not new parent edges, even though they remain part of the original graph. Parent pointers therefore describe the traversal forest rather than copying every graph connection.",
  "For a proper ancestor u of v, DFS enters u first, later enters and finishes v, and only afterward finishes u. The timestamps satisfy pre(u) less than pre(v) less than post(v) less than post(u). This order reflects the waiting behavior of recursive calls and provides a direct check of ancestry in the DFS forest.",
  "A finished vertex has completed its DFS call, so its exit timestamp has already been assigned. It stays marked to prevent another expansion but is no longer on the active stack. Algorithms that distinguish unseen, active, and finished vertices use these states for different purposes. Treating all marked vertices as active would falsely identify cycles in directed acyclic graphs.",
  "To test whether u is a proper ancestor of v, check that u entered earlier and exited later. Both inequalities are needed: entry order alone includes earlier sibling branches, while exit order alone is also insufficient. The test assumes timestamps from the same DFS traversal. It identifies tree ancestry, not every pair connected by a directed path in the graph.",
  "A traversal from one source only discovers vertices reachable from that source. DFSAll adds an outer scan to cover the rest, starting new roots as necessary. This is essential for whole-graph cycle detection or topological sorting, where a missed component could contain important structure. Keep visited marks across these starts so each vertex is expanded once.",
