  "A DAG is a directed graph with no nonempty directed cycle. It may still have an undirected cycle if edge directions prevent going all the way around. This distinction matters for dependency models: directions determine whether tasks depend circularly on one another. Zero-edge paths from a vertex to itself do not count as cycles.",
  "During recursive DFS, active vertices form the current ancestor chain. If an edge from the current vertex returns to an active ancestor, the tree path down from that ancestor plus the new edge forms a directed cycle. The active test is stronger than a visited test, because a finished vertex need not be able to return to the current one.",
  "A source has indegree zero, meaning no edge enters it. In a nonempty DAG, at least one source exists: otherwise repeatedly following incoming edges would eventually revisit a vertex and form a cycle. Sources are natural starting points for topological processing because they have no unsatisfied incoming prerequisites. A graph may have several sources.",
  "A sink has outdegree zero, so no edge leaves it. Every nonempty finite DAG has at least one sink; repeatedly following outgoing edges must stop or produce a cycle. A sink can still have many incoming edges and be reachable from many vertices. The definition concerns direction, not whether the vertex is isolated.",
  "Use three states: unseen, active, and finished. DFS marks a vertex active on entry and finished on return; encountering an edge to an active vertex reports a directed cycle. With adjacency lists, each vertex and outgoing edge is handled a constant number of times, giving O(V plus E). Run the outer DFS scan to cover disconnected or unreachable regions too.",
  "An edge to a finished vertex may simply point into an already explored branch. For example, two branches can both lead to the same completed sink without forming any directed cycle. The cycle witness needs a return to the current active ancestor chain. This is why a directed detector cannot replace the active-state test with 'has ever been visited.'",
  "A directed back edge goes from a vertex to an ancestor whose DFS call is still active. The existing directed tree path runs from that ancestor down to the current vertex, and the back edge closes the loop. A self-loop is the special case where the active target is the current vertex itself.",
  "Finished means the target's DFS call has returned, so it is no longer on the current stack. There may be an edge from the current branch into that earlier region with no path back. Such an edge is not enough to prove a cycle. Use the active-state condition, or another complete cycle argument, rather than treating every non-tree edge as cyclic.",
  "A directed cycle is a nonempty route following arrow directions back to its start. A DAG excludes such cycles, which makes a consistent prerequisite order possible. An underlying undirected loop does not violate the definition if its directions prevent return. State that the route is nonempty so the trivial zero-edge path at every vertex is not mistaken for a cycle.",
  "A source is defined by having no incoming edges. It may have outgoing edges to many successors, making it a natural first vertex in a topological order. A vertex can be both source and sink if isolated. In a nonempty DAG, some source must exist, but arbitrary directed graphs can have none when cycles sustain incoming edges everywhere.",
  "A sink has no outgoing edges, though it may receive edges from other vertices. In a dependency graph with arrows from prerequisite to dependent, a sink has no tasks depending on it. Reversing the graph turns sinks into sources. This directional definition should not be confused with a vertex that happens to be visited last by one traversal.",
  "A boolean visited flag cannot distinguish an unfinished ancestor from a completed branch. Directed cycle detection needs that distinction, so use unseen, active, and finished states or equivalent timestamps and stack membership. An active target closes a cycle; a finished target does not necessarily do so. Each state corresponds to a specific phase of the DFS call.",
  "One back edge supplies an explicit cycle witness: follow parent edges from its active target down to the current vertex, then take the back edge. Therefore a yes-or-no detector can stop immediately when it finds one. If the task asks for all cycles or additional structure, more work is needed, but existence is already established by that single witness.",
  "A complete DFS of a directed graph finds a back edge whenever a directed cycle exists. Therefore, if every vertex is covered and no edge ever targets an active vertex, the graph is acyclic. The coverage condition is essential: a search limited to one source can miss a cycle in an unreachable part of the graph.",
  "When edge u to v targets active ancestor v, the recursion stack contains the tree path from v down to u. Extract that segment and append the edge back to v to report the cycle. Parent pointers can reconstruct the same path. Reporting only the offending edge identifies the trigger but not the complete cycle unless the caller already has the stack context.",
  "DFS expands each vertex once and scans each outgoing adjacency list once. Constant-time state checks on those edges give O(V plus E) total time for whole-graph cycle detection. The bound depends on adjacency lists; scanning a full adjacency-matrix row per vertex can cost O(V squared). Repeatedly launching fresh searches without shared marks would also lose the linear guarantee.",
  "A self-loop goes directly from a vertex back to itself, forming a one-edge directed cycle. During DFS, the vertex is active when its outgoing edges are scanned, so the usual active-target check detects it. Do not ignore this case merely because the cycle has no distinct intermediate vertex; it still prevents a topological ordering.",
  "A cycle can lie outside the vertices reachable from the first DFS source. Finishing that search without a back edge therefore proves only that the explored region has no detected cycle. Use DFSAll to start from every remaining unseen vertex before declaring the entire graph acyclic. The same whole-graph coverage requirement applies to topological sorting.",
