  "The condensation graph has one vertex per SCC and an edge whenever the original graph has an edge between two different components. Internal edges disappear, and parallel component edges can be collapsed if multiplicity is irrelevant. This summarizes the one-way flow between mutually reachable regions and produces a DAG suitable for topological reasoning.",
  "Suppose several distinct SCCs formed a directed cycle in the condensation graph. Traveling around it would let every component reach every other, including a return route. Their vertices would then be mutually reachable and belong to one SCC, contradicting distinctness. This argument explains acyclicity rather than treating it as an arbitrary property of contraction.",
  "In a DAG, two distinct vertices cannot reach each other, because their paths would combine into a directed cycle. Each SCC therefore contains one vertex. Singleton SCCs alone do not rule out self-loops unless those are excluded separately, so be careful when stating the converse. The forward claim from DAG to singleton components is always valid.",
  "A nonempty graph is strongly connected when its entire vertex set is one mutual-reachability class. Computing SCC labels makes this easy to test: all vertices must share the same component. Alternatively, forward and reverse searches from one vertex can both cover every vertex. A single forward search is insufficient because it does not establish return paths.",
