  "A sink SCC has no outgoing connection to a different component, so any path starting inside it stays within it. A traversal from one member therefore visits exactly that SCC. This makes sinks convenient to remove one at a time. The challenge is finding a sink component efficiently, which reversed-graph finishing order solves.",
  "In this version of Kosaraju-Sharir, the first DFS runs on the graph with every edge reversed and records finishing order. The second traversal uses the original graph in decreasing order of those finish times. Another standard presentation swaps the two graphs consistently. Mixing the first pass from one convention with the second pass from the other can break the component-extraction argument.",
  "Process vertices with the latest first-pass finish time first, skipping those already labeled in the second pass. With the first DFS on the reversed graph, this order exposes sink components of the remaining original graph. A traversal can then label one component without spilling into another unlabeled one. The reversal of finishing order is essential to that guarantee.",
