  "A DFS started at one vertex explores only what that vertex can reach. Other disconnected regions, or directed regions unreachable from that source, remain unseen. An outer loop starts new traversals at unmarked vertices to cover the whole graph. Keep marks between starts so the wrapper adds coverage without repeatedly exploring completed regions.",
  "A common way to obtain O(V plus E) is to expand each vertex once and scan each adjacency entry a constant number of times with constant-cost bookkeeping. Repeating a full search for every vertex can multiply the cost. The bound is an accounting claim about total operations; it does not follow merely because each individual loop looks simple.",
  "A DFS tree edge leads from the current vertex to one not yet discovered and initiates its recursive call. Record that current vertex as the new vertex's parent. An edge to an already discovered target is classified differently, even if it connects the same parts of the graph. Discovery status at examination time determines the edge's traversal role.",
