  "A finite tentative label is the cost of a route already found, so the true minimum cannot be larger than it. Infinity represents that no route has been found yet. Relaxation lowers these upper bounds when better routes appear. This invariant depends on initialization and updates corresponding to real walks; arbitrary guessed labels could underestimate the true distance and break the proof.",
  "Edge u to v is tense when the known route to u followed by that edge costs less than v's current label. If dist(u) is four, the edge costs three, and dist(v) is ten, the proposed route costs seven and improves the label. Tension is therefore relative to current distances, not an intrinsic property of the edge weight alone.",
