  "A reachable negative cycle can be traversed repeatedly to reduce a walk's total cost without limit. No finite label can be final for vertices reachable through that cycle. The generic 'relax until no tense edge remains' method therefore cannot reach its stopping condition. Practical algorithms need a detection rule so they can report this situation instead of continuing forever.",
  "The condition compares a concrete proposed route through u against v's current best label. Only a strict improvement changes dist(v) and its parent. This preserves monotonic decreases and records the route responsible for the update. Guard arithmetic involving unreachable labels in implementations that use a finite sentinel for infinity, since overflow could otherwise create a fictitious improvement.",
  "Initialize the source to zero and all other vertices to infinity. Zero represents the empty route to the source, while infinity safely overestimates any as-yet-undiscovered finite route. Every later relaxation uses an existing route plus an edge. Thus finite labels remain costs of actual walks and cannot fall below a finite shortest-path optimum.",
  "An edge's proposed route cost is dist(u) plus its weight. It is tense only if that value is strictly below dist(v). An edge of weight one can be non-tense if u is far away, while a heavier edge can be tense when u is cheaply reachable. Evaluate the full expression rather than deciding from the edge's weight alone.",
  "Relaxation updates the destination v because it has discovered a better way to reach v through u. The tail's label is used but not changed by that operation. Later, the improved value at v may make outgoing edges tense and spread the improvement further. This directional propagation is why relaxation order can affect the number of operations.",
  "When a route through u improves v, recording parent(v) as u identifies the final edge of that improved route. These pointers support path reconstruction once the distance computation is complete. They may change during later improvements, so an intermediate parent chain is not necessarily final. Reachable negative cycles require special handling rather than assuming the pointers will always settle into shortest paths.",
  "The strict comparison permits an update only when the proposed value is smaller than the current one. Finite labels therefore never increase. This monotonicity helps the correctness argument, but it alone does not guarantee termination: a negative cycle permits endlessly decreasing walk costs. The graph's weight structure and the algorithm's stopping or detection rules still matter.",
  "If a reachable cycle has total weight minus two, going around it k times reduces the route cost by 2k. There is no minimum finite walk cost for destinations reachable afterward. This differs from an ordinary negative edge, which can be harmless when no negative cycle exists. The cycle creates unlimited repetition of a cost-reducing action.",
  "An unreachable vertex never receives a finite route from the source. Its label stays infinity because relaxation can only extend routes that already exist. This is the correct answer marker, not an unfinished finite estimate. In code, represent infinity safely and avoid treating a sentinel plus an edge weight as a genuine discovered route.",
