  "With zero edges, the only possible route stays at its starting vertex and costs zero. Distinct endpoints cannot be connected without an edge, so their values are infinity. This base matrix is the starting point for edge-by-edge extension. Giving every pair zero would invent free connections and corrupt all later minima.",
  "An at-most-l route is allowed to use fewer than l edges. Retaining the previous layer's value ensures those already valid routes remain candidates. Without that branch, a recurrence may accidentally describe exactly-l-edge walks instead. The distinction matters when the cheapest route is short and adding an edge would increase its cost or be impossible.",
  "For every incoming edge x to v, combine the best bounded route from u to x with that final edge's weight. The prefix receives one less edge of allowance. Comparing all predecessors covers every possible last edge, while the retained old value covers routes that do not need the extra allowance. This decomposition leads directly to Bellman-Ford-style relaxation.",
  "If a shortest walk repeats a vertex, the repeated segment is a cycle. With no negative cycles, removing that segment cannot increase cost. Repeating the removal yields a simple path with at most V minus one edges. Thus the bounded recurrence captures an optimum at that allowance, while negative cycles would invalidate the finite-optimum reasoning.",
  "An unreachable pair has no route of any edge count, so every bounded state remains infinity. Keep that marker distinct from zero, which is a legitimate route cost. When implementing updates, skip extensions from an infinite prefix or use arithmetic that safely preserves infinity. Otherwise, a large finite sentinel combined with negative weights can mimic a nonexistent route.",
