  "If a layer contains best routes of at most L edges, combining two such routes through every midpoint covers at most 2L edges. Starting from one gives allowances one, two, four, eight, and so on. The doubling is in path length allowance, not in the number of vertices or the distance value. This distinction drives the logarithmic layer count.",
  "Without negative cycles, a shortest path can be chosen with at most V minus one edges. Starting at allowance one and doubling reaches or exceeds that threshold after O(log V) layers. Larger allowances cannot improve a finite optimum through nonnegative cycles. Small graphs need their obvious base handling, such as a single vertex's zero self-distance.",
  "For each ordered pair (u,v), inspect all V possible middle vertices x. There are V squared pairs, so the straightforward triple loop performs Theta(V cubed) combinations per layer. Each combines two stored costs and takes a minimum under the unit-cost model. With logarithmically many layers, this yields O(V cubed log V) time.",
  "The recurrence asks whether a good u-to-v route can be assembled from a bounded u-to-x route and a bounded x-to-v route. It minimizes over every x because the correct midpoint is unknown. For a doubled allowance, both pieces use the preceding layer. This is a different decomposition from choosing only the final predecessor edge.",
