  "Each new layer composes two routes permitted by the previous layer, so its edge allowance doubles. A route using fewer edges is still included because the states mean 'at most,' with empty subroutes available through zero diagonal values. This allows repeated squaring to cover all simple shortest paths after only logarithmically many stages.",
  "The midpoint may be any vertex, including the source or target when one part is empty. Evaluate every choice x and take the minimum of the two part costs added together. Restricting to a guessed middle vertex could miss the best route. The minimization is over graph vertices, not an assumed geometric midpoint or equal-weight split.",
  "Using allowances one, two, four, and so on makes halving exact in the recursive description. For a general odd allowance, additional rounding cases would be needed to ensure both parts cover all paths. Powers of two avoid that bookkeeping without losing the final answer, since any allowance at least V minus one suffices when negative cycles are absent.",
  "Choose the next power of two at or above V minus one. Every simple path fits within that edge allowance, so all finite shortest paths are represented when the graph has no negative cycles. The extra allowance does not harm correctness because the states minimize over routes using at most that many edges, including shorter ones.",
  "After k doublings, the allowance is 2 to the k. Reaching a bound proportional to V therefore requires k proportional to log V. This counts layers only; each layer still performs cubic work in the straightforward implementation. Keeping those two factors separate makes the O(V cubed log V) bound easy to derive and check.",
  "The triple loop chooses a source u, target v, and candidate middle vertex x. Adding the previous-layer costs for u-to-x and x-to-v gives one candidate route cost. Minimizing across x fills one pair entry. There are V choices for each role, producing cubic work per layer even though the number of stored pair entries is only quadratic.",
  "Ordinary matrix multiplication sums products across an index. The shortest-path combination instead adds two route costs and takes the minimum across the middle vertex. This is called min-plus multiplication. Infinity represents missing routes and zero on the diagonal represents empty routes, giving the operations the right behavior for composing and comparing paths.",
  "If no direct edge connects distinct vertices u and v, the one-edge base value is infinity. It should not be zero, which would invent a free connection. Later layers may replace infinity with a finite multi-edge route through a midpoint. For parallel edges, initialize with the lightest direct edge so the base already represents the best allowed one-edge choice.",
