  "Repeated single-source Bellman-Ford extends routes by individual edges. The divide-and-conquer all-pairs method combines two already computed multi-edge routes through a midpoint, doubling the allowance each stage. Both use minima of route costs, but their dependency structure and work counts differ. The min-plus matrix view makes that difference explicit and explains why this method needs only logarithmically many stages.",
