  "A flow network models a resource moving from a source to a sink through limited connections. Each edge restricts how much can pass, while intermediate vertices cannot create or destroy the resource. The question is the greatest total throughput satisfying those rules. The graph may represent pipes or roads, but it can also encode choices in an assignment problem.",
  "Capacity is the maximum permitted flow on an edge, not the amount that must be sent. An edge of capacity five can carry zero, three, or five units in the standard nonnegative model. Other edges may prevent using its full capacity. Feasibility therefore requires checking both edge limits and whether incoming and outgoing amounts balance at intermediate vertices.",
  "The source is the designated vertex where net flow enters the modeled network. Its outgoing flow can exceed its incoming flow, unlike an internal vertex. The difference is the flow value. A graph may contain edges entering the source, so measuring only total outgoing flow would count circulating flow that does not represent additional delivery to the sink.",
  "The sink is the designated destination that absorbs net flow. Its incoming flow minus outgoing flow equals the source's net output because internal vertices conserve flow. This role is part of the model; the sink need not have zero outgoing edges in every formal network. Net quantities handle such edges correctly.",
  "A maximum flow must first be feasible: no edge exceeds capacity and every internal vertex balances inflow with outflow. Among those assignments, choose the largest net source-to-sink value. Filling every edge independently can violate conservation or waste flow on cycles. The objective measures delivered throughput, not the sum of all edge-flow values.",
  "A directed edge represents an allowed direction of movement with a nonnegative upper limit. Paths can split and rejoin at intermediate vertices as long as conservation holds. The network thus captures shared bottlenecks that separate route-by-route calculations might overlook. A maximum-flow algorithm coordinates all edge assignments rather than optimizing each path independently.",
  "The source may produce more outgoing flow than it receives. That net production is what the network attempts to deliver to the sink. Internal vertices have no such privilege: their inflow and outflow must match. Distinguishing these roles prevents a model from accidentally creating extra resource in the middle of a route.",
  "The sink collects the resource delivered through the network. Its net inflow is total incoming flow minus any outgoing flow. Conservation at all other non-source vertices ensures that this equals the source's net outflow. A feasible assignment may include cycles, but those circulations do not increase the amount delivered between the two terminals.",
  "If every source-to-sink route must cross one edge of capacity three, total throughput cannot exceed three, even when all other edges have huge capacities. More generally, a bottleneck can be a set of crossing edges rather than one edge. Cuts formalize that shared restriction and explain why adding capacity elsewhere may not improve the maximum flow.",
  "Capacity constraints keep each edge flow between zero and its limit. Conservation requires every internal vertex's incoming total to equal its outgoing total. Both are necessary: an assignment can respect every edge limit while creating flow at a vertex. The zero assignment satisfies both in the standard model, providing a simple feasible starting point.",
  "With nonnegative upper capacities and no positive lower bounds or mandatory demands, assigning zero to every edge respects all limits and balances every internal vertex. Thus the standard maximum-flow problem always has at least one feasible solution. Variants with required deliveries or lower bounds can lose this property and need a separate feasibility check.",
  "Flow value is outgoing flow at the source minus incoming flow there. If six units leave and two return through a cycle, the net value is four. Counting all six as delivered throughput would overstate progress toward the sink. Conservation makes the same net value appear as sink inflow minus sink outflow.",
  "Suppose an internal vertex receives at most three units but has outgoing edges totaling ten units of capacity. Setting both outgoing edges to their limits would create resource that never arrived. Capacity is only one constraint; conservation couples the edge values. A maximum-flow solver must choose a globally consistent assignment instead of independently maximizing each edge.",
  "An original directed edge permits nonnegative flow from its tail to its head, up to capacity. A backward residual edge does not authorize new physical flow in the opposite direction; it represents canceling some previously assigned forward flow. Keeping original and residual meanings separate makes later rerouting steps easier to understand.",
  "Capacities can represent counts rather than physical transport rates. In bipartite matching, a capacity-one source edge limits a participant to one selected pair. Conservation then connects that resource use to a complete source-to-sink path. A valid reduction must explain how these numerical limits enforce the original problem's rules and how flow value matches its objective.",
  "The returned assignment must satisfy every network constraint and attain the greatest possible value. A large-looking flow is not enough; an upper bound is needed to establish optimality. A cut whose capacity equals the flow value supplies such a certificate. Feasibility shows the value can be achieved, while the cut shows no larger value is possible.",
  "The standard constraint is zero at most f(e) at most c(e). When c(e) is zero, the only feasible value is zero. Such an original edge cannot carry positive flow, though the graph representation may still store it. Residual traversal should use only positive residual capacities, so a zero-capacity connection does not create an augmenting route.",
  "At an internal vertex, everything arriving must leave; the vertex neither supplies nor absorbs net flow. For example, incoming amounts two and three require outgoing amounts totaling five. They need not leave through one edge, so flow can split. This conservation rule is what connects local edge assignments into end-to-end movement from source to sink.",
  "A path's usable capacity is the minimum capacity among its edges. If one edge allows three while the others allow more, sending four along that path would violate the narrow edge. The whole network may still carry more through other routes. Distinguish a single path's bottleneck from the total capacity of all compatible source-to-sink routes.",
  "The edge from source to a can carry at most five units in the original network. It may carry less if downstream capacity is smaller or other routes are preferable. The assignment of a capacity does not itself assign flow. A complete solution must choose edge flows that also conserve resource at a and every other internal vertex."
