  "Each directed edge receives a nonnegative capacity c(e), and feasible flow must stay between zero and that limit. These values define the network's restrictions independently of the algorithm used to solve it. Capacities may be integers or real values, depending on the model. Their representation affects integrality guarantees and the termination analysis of particular augmenting-path implementations.",
  "An internal vertex cannot accumulate or manufacture net flow in the standard steady-state model. Its total incoming amount therefore equals its total outgoing amount. Several incoming streams can merge or one stream can split across outgoing edges. Conservation checks totals, not a one-to-one pairing of individual incoming and outgoing edges.",
  "An edge of capacity c permits at most c units of flow. Saturating it means assigning exactly c, while leaving it unused means assigning zero. A feasible maximum flow need not saturate every edge because bottlenecks elsewhere can limit delivery. Capacity is a local upper bound within a globally constrained optimization problem.",
  "Sum conservation equations over all internal vertices and the internal transfers cancel. What remains is the source's net output balancing the sink's net input. Thus flow cannot disappear between the terminals. This equality uses net quantities, so it remains valid when the graph has edges entering the source or leaving the sink.",
  "Subtracting source inflow avoids counting returned circulation as new throughput. If outgoing edges carry eight and incoming edges carry three, the flow value is five. The code assumes outgoing and incoming contain actual flow amounts, not capacities. Conservation elsewhere then makes this value equal to the sink's net received amount.",
  "Zero on every edge obeys nonnegative capacity limits and gives equal zero inflow and outflow at every internal vertex. It is therefore a valid starting flow for the standard problem. This relies on having no positive lower-flow requirements or compulsory demands. Those variants require additional feasibility machinery and cannot assume the zero assignment works.",
  "The interval from zero to capacity describes every allowed amount on one original directed edge. A negative value would mean sending flow against its defined direction, while a value above capacity would exceed its resource limit. Residual algorithms represent cancellation separately through backward residual edges, so original edge-flow variables can remain nonnegative throughout.",
  "Check conservation by summing all flows entering an internal vertex and all flows leaving it. Equality ensures the vertex neither creates nor absorbs resource. An edge-wise capacity check cannot establish this condition. For example, two legal outgoing flows of three each are still infeasible if the vertex receives only four units in total.",
  "The source's net output is the amount it sends beyond what returns to it. This quantity measures useful source-to-sink throughput rather than all circulation in the graph. A cycle carrying two units through the source contributes equally to incoming and outgoing totals and cancels. That is why the definition uses a difference instead of merely summing outgoing edges.",
  "Every edge's flow leaves one endpoint and enters another. Summing net flow over all vertices therefore gives zero. Internal vertices contribute zero by conservation, leaving source net outflow equal to sink net inflow. This accounting identity is also what makes the net flow across any source-sink cut equal the total flow value.",
  "Sending the same amount around a directed cycle preserves conservation at each cycle vertex. It adds no net source-to-sink throughput because the flow returns to where it started. Such circulation may consume capacity without improving the objective. Removing a positive-flow cycle can simplify a flow while leaving its value unchanged.",
  "Capacity states what an edge may carry, not what it must carry. If an edge allows ten units but only four can reach its tail, sending four or fewer may be the feasible choice. Requiring every edge to be full would define a different and often infeasible problem. Maximum flow maximizes net delivery while allowing unused capacity.",
  "A flow is a network-wide assignment, so verify every internal vertex rather than only the ones on a favored path. A hidden imbalance elsewhere can invalidate the whole result even when source and sink totals look plausible. Capacity and conservation checks together establish feasibility. Optimality then needs a separate argument, such as an equal-valued cut.",
  "A single path must carry the same amount through each of its edges to conserve flow along its internal vertices. The smallest edge capacity therefore limits that amount. For capacities eight, three, and six, the path supports at most three units. Additional paths can increase total network flow if their shared capacities are accounted for consistently.",
  "If no directed source-to-sink path exists, no positive amount can be delivered between them. A cut separating all source-reachable vertices from the sink has zero forward capacity, certifying the upper bound zero. The all-zero flow achieves it. Cycles elsewhere may carry circulation but cannot create positive net delivery to the unreachable sink.",
  "Parallel edges are distinct resources, each with its own flow and capacity constraint. Two parallel edges of capacities two and three can jointly carry five units if the rest of the network permits it. A data structure keyed only by endpoint pair may accidentally overwrite one edge. Preserve edge identity or deliberately combine capacities when that transformation is appropriate.",
  "Net source outflow cannot exceed total source outflow, which cannot exceed the sum of capacities leaving the source. This gives an immediate upper bound on the maximum value. It may be loose because a smaller bottleneck occurs downstream. The source cut is one member of the family of cut bounds, not necessarily the minimum cut.",
  "Net sink inflow is at most total inflow, and each incoming edge is limited by its capacity. Summing those capacities therefore bounds every feasible flow value. A narrow connection elsewhere may give a tighter cut bound. This simple terminal bound is useful for checking results and choosing safe finite capacities in reductions.",
  "Feasibility means an assignment obeys capacities and conservation. Optimality means no other feasible assignment has greater value. The zero flow is often feasible but far from maximum. An augmenting residual path proves a feasible flow can be improved, while a cut of equal capacity proves that further improvement is impossible.",
  "Integral capacities guarantee the existence of an integral maximum flow and support integer augmentations from zero. Real capacities still define a valid mathematical maximum-flow problem, but arbitrary augmenting-path choices can have different termination behavior, especially with irrational values. State the number model and chosen algorithm instead of assuming every flow procedure inherits the same runtime guarantee."
